Revenue models describe how customers pay; cost structures explain resources consumed.|A subscription firm earns recurring fees but incurs support and acquisition costs.|Revenue growth alone establishes profitable economics?|No|Yes|Only for subscriptions
Fixed costs stay stable within a relevant range; variable costs scale with activity; mixed costs contain both.|Cost = ₹100 fixed + ₹2 per unit.|At 50 units, total cost is…?|₹200|₹100|₹102
Contribution covers fixed costs after variable costs; break-even occurs when total contribution equals fixed costs.|Price ₹10 − variable cost ₹6 gives ₹4 contribution; ₹100 fixed costs require 25 units.|Break-even volume is…?|25 units|10 units|40 units
Price-volume-mix separates revenue changes into pricing, quantity, and product composition.|Selling more premium products can raise average revenue without changing individual prices.|Which driver changed?|Mix|Only price|Only tax
Higher utilisation spreads fixed capacity costs; operating leverage magnifies profit changes.|Unused factory capacity adds fixed cost without proportional revenue.|A demand fall can hurt high-fixed-cost earnings…?|Disproportionately|Only proportionally|Not at all
Scale lowers unit cost with size; scope lowers cost through joint activities.|One distribution network serves two product lines.|This illustrates…?|Economies of scope|Pure currency hedging|Debt amortisation
CAC measures relevant acquisition spending per new customer.|₹1,000 acquisition spending for 100 customers gives CAC ₹10.|Spending ₹1,200 for 100 customers gives…?|₹12|₹120|₹0.12
LTV estimates discounted customer contribution over the relationship; revenue alone is insufficient.|₹20 annual contribution for three years has undiscounted value ₹60 before acquisition costs.|Should LTV use revenue while ignoring delivery costs?|No|Yes|Only for new customers
Retention measures continuing customers; churn measures losses; cohorts track shared start periods.|100 starting customers lose 10: customer churn is 10%, excluding new acquisitions.|New sign-ups erase the old cohort's churn?|No|Yes|Only in SaaS
Customer and product profitability allocate revenues and economically relevant servicing costs.|Customer A generates ₹20 margin but requires ₹25 bespoke support.|Its contribution after that support is…?|−₹5|₹20|₹45
Acquisition payback compares CAC with subsequent contribution, respecting timing and retention.|CAC ₹120 and monthly contribution ₹20 imply six months before other effects.|Payback is…?|6 months|12 months|2 months
Working-capital intensity measures operating cash tied up; capital intensity measures long-lived investment needs.|Two firms have equal sales but different inventory and machinery requirements.|Equal margins imply equal free cash flow?|No|Yes|Only at equal sales
Network effects strengthen user value as participation grows; financial benefits depend on monetisation and costs.|A marketplace attracts buyers through seller variety but subsidises transactions.|User growth automatically proves cash profitability?|No|Yes|Only for marketplaces
Pricing and discounts influence demand, contribution, and customer behaviour.|Price ₹10 with ₹6 variable cost yields ₹4 contribution; a ₹1 discount reduces contribution to ₹3.|Contribution falls by…?|25%|10%|1%
Driver models connect operational activity with financial results.|Revenue = customers × purchases per customer × average price.|Which is a revenue driver?|Purchase frequency|Depreciation method only|Debt maturity only
